courses.learning_outcomes:
Upon completion of the course, students should be able to

(1) understand typical designs in biomedical studies;
(2) use descriptive statistics to explore data;
(3) choose appropriate statistical tests, implement then test and interpret their results;
(4) apply and interpret methods for comparison and association analyses;
(5) perform basic analysis for survival data analysis and interpret the results; and

(6) calculate statistical power and determine sample size.

Upon completion of the course, students should be able to
(1) design a marketing research to support a marketing decision;
(2) design questionnaires in a scientific manner;
(3) determine the value of marketing information;
(4) use network analysis to manage a marketing research or other project.

Upon completion of the course, students should be able to
(1) comprehend standard generation methods for uniform and nonuniform random numbers;
(2) understand the advantages and disadvantages of computer simulation;
(3) acquire experience in problem solving using simulation;
(4) apply simulation to statistical estimation, hypotheses testing, and model selection;
(5) learn statistical software R and its uses in simulation and statistical computing.

Upon course completion, students should be able to
(1) communicate research ideas with their supervisor;
(2) conduct a literature review;
(3) perform empirical studies; and
(4) develop new theories or methodologies.
